Changes relative to Lucis 2.0 (M0)
----------------------------------

Identity
  * New mod id `lucistarlink`, display name `LuciStarlink`, package `dev.lucistarlink`, class prefix `Lux*`,
    entry class `LuciStarlink`. Resource, mixin config, asset namespace, benchmark metric keys, system
    properties (`lucistarlink.*`) and benchmark run directories renamed accordingly.

Memory safety
  * `RegionLightData.byteSize()` added; `OwnedRegionCache.trimToSize(entries, bytes)` now bounds the region
    cache by memory as well as by entry count (a single region is megabytes, so the entry cap was not a real
    bound). At least one region is always retained.
  * `LuxConfig.maxCachedRegionMegabytes` (default 256 MiB) plus `-Dlucistarlink.maxCachedRegionMegabytes=`.
  * `RuntimeUpdateQueue.enqueueFullRelight` now returns false once the region table reaches
    `maxPendingRecords` instead of growing without bound; refusal is counted, and existing regions are still
    accepted.
  * `LuxRuntimeManager.close()` also clears the drained-batch scratch map.

Correctness / lifecycle
  * `runtimeHaloChunks` config (default 1) wired into runtime jobs; `0` logs a warning explaining that
    cross-chunk light propagation is truncated at region borders.
  * `LuxEngineController` gained a `closed` flag: disabled hooks degrade to vanilla lighting, `shutdown()` is
    idempotent and clears its thread locals, `relightChunkAsync` no longer risks rejection after shutdown.
  * Leaked runtime bulk-write scopes are reaped after `lucistarlink.runtime.bulk.maxIdleNanos` (default 30 s)
    with a warning, instead of silently suppressing all later block-change light updates on that thread.
  * When runtime updates are disabled, already-computed commits are still flushed so their chunk references
    are released.

Observability
  * Boot log reports the region cache budget; `verboseLogging` prints a periodic memory telemetry line for the
    region cache, coalescing map, queues, pending batches, commits and scheduled regions.

Not yet ported (roadmap, see docs/roadmap-and-provenance.md)
  * Heightmap/column-driven sky propagation (M1), save/load and mod-compat hardening (M2), intra-job
    parallelism and client-side support (M3), and a correct cross-region boundary continuation to replace the
    disabled `experimentalBoundaryDeltas` prototype (M2).

Changes for cross-region light correctness (M2, runtime path)
-------------------------------------------------------------

* Halo publication (`haloPublish`, default on): a job now publishes the dirty sections of its halo chunks in
  addition to its owned chunk, so light computed across a region border reaches the neighbouring chunk
  immediately. Previously only the owned chunk was published, which left a dark seam at chunk borders until
  something else touched the neighbouring region. Cost is proportional to the light that actually crossed
  (dirty-section tracking), and `LuxPublishEngine.publishChunk` now accepts any chunk inside the image
  instead of silently refusing everything outside the core.
* External-section refresh: regions whose image overlaps a published chunk are marked, and re-read exactly
  those sections from the engine before their next job uses the image as a baseline. This keeps every region
  image consistent with the engine, which is what the disabled cross-region delta prototype
  (`experimentalBoundaryDeltas`) failed to do without oscillating.
* Baseline-moved guard: a job records the external-mark epoch after refreshing and, if it moved while the job
  ran, re-queues the batch instead of publishing values derived from a stale baseline. Counted as
  `lucistarlink.runtime.jobs.rerunBaselineMoved`.
* Fixed (inherited): the adoption-backed region init discarded the batch's own block changes, so a light
  source placed in a region that had no prior runtime job never lit up (the engine is bypassed for those
  changes, so adoption alone cannot contain them). Adoption now applies the batch on top of the adopted
  baseline. Counted as `lucistarlink.runtime.init.adoptedChanges`.
* Telemetry extended with halo/external/rerun counters; the effective flags are logged at boot.

Save-side light safety (M2b)
----------------------------

* New mixin `ChunkSerializerMixin` redirects the `isLightCorrect` read in `ChunkSerializer.write`: a chunk whose
  region still has queued or in-flight engine work is written as light-not-correct, so the game relights it on
  load. Nothing relights a chunk that loads as light-correct, so without this a chunk saved during a pending
  light update (place a torch, quit immediately) would come back with that light permanently missing.
* `forceLightIncorrectOnSave` (default off) forces the flag for *every* chunk, which is what ScalableLux ships.
  It additionally covers a chunk whose neighbour was generated after it was saved, at the cost of relighting
  every chunk on load. The precise mode is the default because it costs nothing on unaffected chunks.
* Counters: `lucistarlink.save.forcedLightIncorrect.pendingWork` / `.global` (surfaced in the telemetry line).

Changes after M2b: lazy materialisation, publish-path switches, measurement
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Lazy materialisation (performance, shipped)
  * Halo light is materialised per section on demand (`-Dlucistarlink.lazyHaloLight=`, system property only,
    default on) instead of building the whole region image up front: region init 435.3 -> 0.87 ms.
  * Halo materials are likewise extracted per section on demand (`LuxRegionExtractor.populateSection`,
    `materializeReach`): extraction 56.8 -> 5.6-13.9 ms.

Publish-path switches (all default off, all measured, none promoted)
  * `directSectionInstall` - install a computed section straight into the engine's storage. A sequential group
    once measured -31% on `sky_hole`, which did **not** reproduce under same-session interleaved A/B
    (`sky_hole` -13%, `dense` flat, `block_toggle_border` +4%, `structure_cube` -3%; no workload significant),
    so that gain was group drift. Not enabled: it also skips the engine's re-check of handed-over sections,
    which is what keeps a stale computed image from winning.
  * `piggybackPublish` (worse), `promptRuntimePublish` (worse, +44% - the 250 us publish coalescing window is
    a benefit, not a cost), `syncRuntimeDrain` (neutral), `worldgenHaloPublish` (default **on**; switching it
    off trades a possible border seam for handing far fewer sections to the light engine).

Measurement methodology (treated as part of the deliverable)
  * Accepted statistic: **median of per-pass minima, >=5 reps, same-session interleaved run** (our run and the
    compared engine's alternate round by round). The same configuration measured 0.651 / 0.767 / 1.069 in three
    groups of one session, so absolute numbers are only comparable within a group; cross-group comparison is
    what produced two disagreeing tables earlier.
  * Each benchmark row records its label and effective switches in `lucistarlink-light-benchmark.jsonl`, so a
    third party can regroup without trusting any conclusion written here.
  * Tables should carry Mann-Whitney U/p: at n=5 with this spread, medians alone overstate narrow differences.
  * Client-sync case verified: light placed on a chunk border appears on a connected dev client within 2 s,
    on both sides of the border, with no reconnect or chunk reload - measured on full-resolution in-game
    screenshots (dark / lit / dark frames plus a difference image), archived under mc-smoketest/clientcase-evidence.
  * Withdrawn claims: the "7 pairs / 6 divergent" cross-region block-light tally and the statement that
    in-place `DataLayer` overwrite fixed it were both retracted. The divergence investigation closed clean
    (7 controlled runs, 0 divergence, cell-identical plane diff); in-place overwrite is a requirement derived
    from the thread model (the light thread keeps writing into the object it holds), not a proven fix.
    See docs/NOTE-TO-SUPERVISOR.md section 2.

Not done / not pending
  * Client-side lighting takeover and intra-job parallelism (M3) - not started.
  * Interoperability measurement with worldgen optimizers (C2ME, Generator Accelerator) - never executed.
  * `experimentalBoundaryDeltas` and its `BorderDeltaSupport` class are dead code slated for deletion.
  * Worldgen border ordering: a chunk generated at the edge of loaded terrain publishes only itself; the
    conservative `forceLightIncorrectOnSave=true` covers the residual case.
  * Heightmap/column-driven sky propagation (M1) was evaluated and **rejected** as a non-lever; it is not
    "pending" as the M0 section above lists it.

